[Music]  if you've ever dreamt of giving up your  day job to move to the beach well you're  not alone and that's exactly what the  owners of little ram oysters did they  gave up their desk jobs in manhattan and  bought an oyster farm that's now owned  and operated entirely by women so today  they're going to show me how to shuck  some oysters and how oysters get from  the water to our plates  [Music]  i'm stephanie bassett and i'm elizabeth  peeples and we own little ram oyster  company  we are an all-female team owned and  operated  and we are off  and we're heading east to the east side  of shelter island to gardner's bed  and that's where our farm is  so beautiful  my background is as an interior designer  and stuff was in advertising and all of  a sudden we were just kind of trying to  decide what was next we got a groupon  and one evening we were taking a  shucking class  we already knew we loved oysters and we  just learned so much how to shuck them  how to taste them you know all the taste  profiles how great they are for the  environment and then six months later we  actually bought a house we had a kid and  we bought an oyster farm and here we are  [Music]  so right now we are in the middle of  their 10 acre oyster farm and they  harvest about 1.5 million oysters a year  we touch every oyster every six weeks we  picked up some cages that needed to be  worked on and we emptied them into the  tumbler  it roughs them up so it knocks all that  new shell off and what that does is it  forces the oyster to tighten its muscle  which strengthens the shell which gives  it a deeper cuff and then by doing that  every six weeks you get a more  consistent shape you have to do that  every six weeks with every single one of  those 1.5 million oysters yeah oh my god  and it takes about 12 to 18 months for  our oysters to  go to market so we love them all very  much  so this is a good example of like the  deep cup that stuff was talking about  you want it you want this because this  is a meteor oyster  right now we're putting them back in the  water so they can keep threatening and  eating and growing and they're clean and  they're good to go we'll touch them in  six weeks  [Music]  one thing that is impacting us majorly  right now are the gas prices because we  usually go out to the farm twice a day  so that's four trips so that is hitting  us  we knew it was going to be a challenge  we knew it was part of farming but just  the weather and the tides and the water  movement that drives our every single  day of work always at the mercy of  mother nature mother nature is huge and  she reminds us about that every single  day it's a good reminder  i knew that they were  delicious and i loved eating oysters but  i had no idea they were good for the  environment  they are filter feeders they filter 50  gallons of water a day  they sequester carbon and nitrogen from  the water column and so they are  actively working to clean the water and  improve the environment  so the more oysters you have in the  water whether you like them or not it's  doing good for everybody yeah  and then the next  chapter of little ram which is  our new home operation here at the  shoals  what we can now offer is this kind of  experiential aspect it's a really great  collaboration a lot of the hotel guests  come over and you know experience you  know private tours we offer shucking  classes we do a shuck truck for a happy  hour it's been a great kind of next  chapter for us  we typically shuck through  the side like here or the back we just  feel it's a cleaner way of getting in  so you gotta  cut through that top muscle release it  underneath too and that creates that  loose oyster  when you eat an oyster you should always  be looking out for a lot of juice it's  an oyster liquor you don't ever want  them to be dry you definitely don't want  them to smell  they should  have almost no smell to them  you're looking for a meat that covers  the entire shell you want that chuck  clean you're spending a lot of money on  it and the farmers are spending a lot of  time on it  the other thing that's important to us  is to connect with the community  it's how we've grown our business by  everybody who has supported us along the  way and the other new york oyster  farmers who have kind of taken our hand  and pulled us along and taught us the  techniques  you know what used to be all men it's  pretty cool being able to put five women  on the water and growing that and seeing  how the community and visitors are  reacting to it and it's fun to see how  it's all working out  just a little lemon just a couple drops  i like a little pepper cheers cheers  what a fun yeah  cheers cheers  wow it tastes like vacation  [Music]  you

